00:57:28.690 --> 00:57:30.640
because the light interacts--

00:57:30.640 --> 00:57:34.540
the interaction will
be time dependent.

00:57:34.540 --> 00:57:37.690
But for the moment, I
want to find the only,

00:57:37.690 --> 00:57:41.330
given a potential, what's
the energy of the system.

00:57:41.330 --> 00:57:46.750
So I say a space dependent,
but time independent potential.

00:57:46.750 --> 00:57:50.920
And in that case, if you go look
at your Schrodinger equation,

00:57:50.920 --> 00:57:51.820
you say here--

00:57:54.980 --> 00:57:56.540
this is a time dependent.

00:57:56.540 --> 00:57:58.250
This one is space.

00:57:58.250 --> 00:58:02.510
This one is space, so I can
use the separation of variable

00:58:02.510 --> 00:58:06.540
to separate the
time and space part.

00:58:06.540 --> 00:58:11.490
So I'm going to
write, in this case,

00:58:11.490 --> 00:58:15.890
I'm going to say, time,
space, into a separation

00:58:15.890 --> 00:58:21.125
of variable space
and time function.

00:58:24.730 --> 00:58:27.250
And if we substitute
into the equation--

00:58:44.770 --> 00:58:49.260
So if you stop into this
equation, what you find

00:58:49.260 --> 00:59:02.910
is I have psi h squared 2m
the Laplace operator psi

00:59:02.910 --> 00:59:15.420
plus u psi equals i
h bar y and dy dt.

00:59:15.420 --> 00:59:19.530
So this is the same as we solve
any separation or variable

00:59:19.530 --> 00:59:20.520
problem.

00:59:20.520 --> 00:59:22.870
This is space dependent only.

00:59:22.870 --> 00:59:24.690
This is a time dependent only.

00:59:24.690 --> 00:59:26.700
So the only way they
equal to each other

00:59:26.700 --> 00:59:31.090
is each of them is a constant
